page2 = f"""
<h2>3.2.2 95% Confidence Interval (c)</h2>

<p>
A confidence interval provides a range of plausible values for
the unknown population mean house sale price. A 95% confidence
level is used in this analysis.
</p>

<p>
Because the population standard deviation is unknown, the sample
standard deviation is used. The Student's t confidence interval
is expressed as:
</p>

<div class="formula">
95% CI = x̄ ± t(0.025, n−1) × (s / √n)
</div>

<p>Where:</p>
<ul>
<li>x̄ is the sample mean;</li>
<li>t(0.025, n−1) is the critical value from the Student's t distribution;</li>
<li>s is the sample standard deviation;</li>
<li>n is the sample size.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Calculation</h3>

<p><strong>Step 1. Standard Error (SE)</strong></p>

<div class="formula">
SE = s / √n
</div>

<p>
SE = {usd(std_price)} / √{n:,}
= <strong>{usd(standard_error)}</strong>
</p>

<p>
The standard error measures the estimated variability of the
sample mean across repeated samples.
</p>

<p><strong>Step 2. Critical value</strong></p>

<p>
Degrees of freedom = n − 1 = <strong>{df:,}</strong>.
The critical value is approximately <strong>{number(t_critical)}</strong>.
A large-sample approximation is used because the sample size
is very large.
</p>

<p><strong>Step 3. Margin of Error (ME)</strong></p>

<div class="formula">
ME = t critical value × SE
</div>

<p>
ME = {number(t_critical)} × {usd(standard_error)}
= <strong>{usd(margin_error)}</strong>
</p>

<p><strong>Step 4. Confidence interval limits</strong></p>

<div class="formula">
Lower limit = x̄ − ME
</div>
<p>
= {usd(mean_price)} − {usd(margin_error)}
= <strong>{usd(lower_ci)}</strong>
</p>

<div class="formula">
Upper limit = x̄ + ME
</div>
<p>
= {usd(mean_price)} + {usd(margin_error)}
= <strong>{usd(upper_ci)}</strong>
</p>

<div class="result">
<strong>95% Confidence Interval</strong>
<p>({usd(lower_ci)}, {usd(upper_ci)})</p>
</div>

